WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.490
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.979
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:18.073
Unit forty-one: Food, Culture and Identity. In this unit, you will discuss food as a mirror of culture and identity.

4
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:20.491
culinary heritage.

5
00:00:21.191 --> 00:00:27.161
culinary heritage means the traditional dishes and cooking knowledge passed down in a culture.

6
00:00:27.461 --> 00:00:32.026
Amok and samlor kako are part of Cambodia's culinary heritage.

7
00:00:32.726 --> 00:00:36.307
Your turn. Say: culinary heritage.

8
00:00:39.007 --> 00:00:40.380
culinary heritage.

9
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:42.593
gastronomy.

10
00:00:43.293 --> 00:00:46.895
gastronomy means the art and study of good food and cooking.

11
00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:50.669
Siem Reap is becoming a centre of Khmer gastronomy.

12
00:00:51.369 --> 00:00:54.486
Your turn. Say: gastronomy.

13
00:00:57.186 --> 00:00:58.199
gastronomy.

14
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:00.463
food culture.

15
00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:06.715
food culture means the beliefs, habits and customs connected with food in a society.

16
00:01:07.015 --> 00:01:11.871
Sharing dishes from the centre of the table is part of Khmer food culture.

17
00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:15.641
Your turn. Say: food culture.

18
00:01:18.341 --> 00:01:19.400
food culture.

19
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:21.313
terroir.

20
00:01:22.013 --> 00:01:27.449
terroir means the way local soil, climate and tradition give food a unique taste.

21
00:01:27.749 --> 00:01:30.446
Kampot pepper owes its flavour to its terroir.

22
00:01:31.146 --> 00:01:33.368
Your turn. Say: terroir.

23
00:01:36.068 --> 00:01:36.884
terroir.

24
00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:38.989
comfort food.

25
00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:45.218
comfort food means simple food that makes people feel safe, warm and at home.

26
00:01:45.518 --> 00:01:48.446
Rice porridge is my comfort food when I am ill.

27
00:01:49.146 --> 00:01:51.833
Your turn. Say: comfort food.

28
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:55.616
comfort food.

29
00:01:56.617 --> 00:01:57.641
foodways.

30
00:01:58.341 --> 00:02:03.893
foodways means the cultural practices around producing, cooking and eating food.

31
00:02:04.193 --> 00:02:08.248
Studying foodways reveals how families and festivals work.

32
00:02:08.948 --> 00:02:12.146
Your turn. Say: foodways.

33
00:02:14.846 --> 00:02:15.754
foodways.

34
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.297
culinary diplomacy.

35
00:02:18.997 --> 00:02:24.027
culinary diplomacy means using food to build understanding between nations.

36
00:02:24.327 --> 00:02:28.636
Thailand's culinary diplomacy helped spread Thai restaurants worldwide.

37
00:02:29.336 --> 00:02:32.918
Your turn. Say: culinary diplomacy.

38
00:02:35.618 --> 00:02:37.304
culinary diplomacy.

39
00:02:38.317 --> 00:02:39.550
fusion cuisine.

40
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.409
fusion cuisine means cooking that combines traditions from different cultures.

41
00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:47.742
A prahok pizza would be daring fusion cuisine.

42
00:02:48.442 --> 00:02:51.895
Your turn. Say: fusion cuisine.

43
00:02:54.595 --> 00:02:55.910
fusion cuisine.

44
00:02:56.917 --> 00:02:59.090
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.594
Ms. Sarah, why is Cambodian food less famous than Thai or Vietnamese food?

46
00:03:05.883 --> 00:03:10.054
Not until recently did Cambodia invest in culinary diplomacy.

47
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:13.779
But our culinary heritage is ancient!

48
00:03:15.083 --> 00:03:20.162
Indeed. Rarely does a cuisine have such deep roots, from Angkor to the Mekong.

49
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.367
And Kampot pepper has its own protected terroir.

50
00:03:25.683 --> 00:03:31.085
What makes Khmer food special is its balance: sour, salty, sweet and bitter.

51
00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:35.184
Should young chefs experiment with fusion cuisine?

52
00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:41.583
It is creativity rooted in tradition that we need, not novelty for its own sake.

53
00:03:42.883 --> 00:03:48.261
Grammar focus: Advanced inversion and emphasis (extended).

54
00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.768
Inversion and clefts add emphasis.

55
00:03:52.068 --> 00:03:58.900
Not until recently did Cambodia invest in culinary diplomacy. Rarely does a cuisine have such deep roots.

56
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.384
What makes Khmer food special is its balance.

57
00:04:02.684 --> 00:04:10.593
Inversion and cleft sentences highlight new or important information; use them for emphasis, not in every sentence.

58
00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:14.329
Now read and listen. You Are What You Eat.

59
00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:19.426
Food is never only fuel; it is a language through which cultures speak.

60
00:04:20.717 --> 00:04:27.709
Cambodia's culinary heritage reflects centuries of exchange with India, China and its neighbours.

61
00:04:29.017 --> 00:04:34.708
Its food culture values balance, freshness and sharing, with many dishes served together.

62
00:04:36.017 --> 00:04:43.032
Products such as Kampot pepper, protected by a geographical indication, show the importance of terroir.

63
00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:50.577
Historians study foodways to understand family life, religious festivals and economic change.

64
00:04:51.883 --> 00:04:56.542
For migrants, comfort food becomes a powerful link to home and identity.

65
00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:04.087
Rarely has food travelled as quickly as today, and fusion cuisine is now found in every capital.

66
00:05:05.383 --> 00:05:11.493
Some governments even practise culinary diplomacy, promoting national dishes to win friends abroad.

67
00:05:12.783 --> 00:05:15.401
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:15.701 --> 00:05:20.127
The way local soil and climate give food a unique taste is blank.

69
00:05:20.427 --> 00:05:25.415
A, terroir. B, gastronomy. C, foodways.

70
00:05:29.417 --> 00:05:31.683
The answer is A: terroir.

71
00:05:33.183 --> 00:05:35.972
Rarely blank a cuisine have such deep roots.

72
00:05:36.272 --> 00:05:39.043
A, does. B, do. C, is.

73
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.235
The answer is A: does.

74
00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.979
'What makes Khmer food special is its balance' is a blank.

75
00:05:51.279 --> 00:05:56.975
A, wh-cleft sentence. B, passive sentence. C, conditional.

76
00:06:00.983 --> 00:06:04.713
The answer is A: wh-cleft sentence.

77
00:06:06.217 --> 00:06:09.714
Using food to build understanding between nations is blank.

78
00:06:10.014 --> 00:06:16.129
A, culinary diplomacy. B, fusion cuisine. C, comfort food.

79
00:06:20.117 --> 00:06:23.022
The answer is A: culinary diplomacy.

80
00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:27.944
According to the text, what protects Kampot pepper?

81
00:06:28.244 --> 00:06:35.102
A, a geographical indication. B, a royal decree. C, a trade embargo.

82
00:06:39.117 --> 00:06:42.567
The answer is A: a geographical indication.

83
00:06:44.083 --> 00:06:49.650
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can Cambodia make its cuisine better known in the world?

84
00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:59.318
Part two: Use one negative inversion and one cleft sentence.

85
00:07:05.618 --> 00:07:16.850
Not until we train more chefs will Khmer food become famous. What we need is a national strategy: festivals abroad, cooking schools and protected products like Kampot pepper.

86
00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:19.463
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:27.410
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced inversion and emphasis (extended).

88
00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:32.347
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:32.647 --> 00:07:35.474
See you in Unit forty-two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:35.774 --> 00:07:38.287
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

